## 1. Faulty Ignition Coils

The ignition coil is one of the most critical components in any car, as it is responsible for igniting the air-fuel mixture that powers the engine. Unfortunately, faulty ignition coils are a common BMW car issue and can cause engine misfires, stalls, and reduced power.

As you can imagine, this is a major safety concern that should be addressed immediately. So, if you suspect that your BMW’s ignition coil is faulty, you should have it checked by a manufacturer-certified mechanic for diagnosis and repair.

If the problem persists after multiple repair attempts - and your car is covered under warranty - it may qualify for BMW lemon law. Contact a [California lemon law attorney](/) to learn more about your legal options.

## 2. Electric Window Problems

Many BMW owners have also reported problems with their electric windows, particularly with the window getting stuck in the downright position. This common BMW problem may allow outside elements to enter your car, which can potentially damage the interior.

Generally, the problem with electric window issues is a subpar installation of the motor responsible for operating the windows. The best way to address these BMW car issues is by having a BMW-certified mechanic inspect and replace the faulty parts on your vehicle.

Don’t settle for a temporary repair if your vehicle is affected by this issue. Since faulty windows interfere with the functionality/use of your car, a reoccurring problem like this may qualify you for a BMW lemon law case.

## 3. Dead Batteries

Another common BMW problem that can impact the safety and performance of your car is faulty or dead batteries. BMW batteries have a reputation for reliability issues, and many owners complain that they only last a few years before needing to be replaced.

If you experience a dead battery in your BMW, the first step is to check the condition of the battery terminals. Corroded or loose connections are often the culprit for dead batteries and can easily be fixed with a little cleaning and tightening. However, if this does not solve your BMW car issue, it may be necessary to have your battery tested or replaced by a mechanic.

Again, if this problem is chronic and arises under warranty, you may qualify for BMW lemon law protection.

## 4. Automatic Headlight Issues

Many BMW owners have reported experiencing problems with their car’s automatic headlights, such as not working or turning on at random times. This common BMW problem can be a significant safety hazard and may result in an accident if you are driving at night without adequate lighting.

It’s important to get faulty headlights checked by a certified mechanic as soon as possible if you are having this issue with your BMW. These defects pose a large risk to you and other drivers. A professional should perform the necessary repairs and ensure that your car’s automatic headlights are working optimally.

If you continue to have issues with your automatic headlights despite repairs, it may qualify for a BMW [lemon law claim](/info/everything-you-need-to-know-about-california-lemon-law-claims). Be sure to document any repair attempts and correspondence with the manufacturer. This will be crucial information for your BMW lemon law case.

## 5. Coolant System Failures

Coolant systems are responsible for keeping your car’s engine at a safe operating temperature, and they also prevent overheating. If the coolant system in your BMW fails, it can cause extensive damage to the engine and may even necessitate a replacement.

Some of the most common signs of this common BMW problem include leaking coolant, poor heat output from the heater, and engine overheating. If you suspect that your BMW’s coolant system may be failing, it is important to have it serviced as soon as possible.

Contact a California lemon law attorney if you own a BMW that has been subjected to multiple coolant system repairs, but the problem still persists. You may be entitled to compensation under BMW lemon law.

## 6. Leaky Oil Filter Gasket

Another common BMW car issue is a [leaky oil filter gasket](https://www.valvoline.com/en-eur/engine-oil-leak-causes-and-how-to-fix-them). The oil filter helps to regulate the flow of oil in your engine, and a leaky gasket can cause excessive oil loss. This can eventually lead to significant engine damage.

If your BMW’s oil filter appears wet or oily, or if you notice a strong burning odor near the oil filter, you should have it checked by a mechanic right away. They can replace the oil filter gasket relatively quickly.

If the problem can’t be resolved through repairs, you may be eligible for a BMW lemon law case.

